# Google SoC drivers
#

config GOOGLE_AOC
	tristate "Google AOC (Always-On Compute) coprocessor"
	depends on EXYNOS_GSA
	help
	  The AOC is an always-on coprocessor on Google Tensor SoCs that owns
	  Bluetooth, audio and the sensor hub.  The GSA security core
	  authenticates its firmware, so the driver needs it.

	  Releasing the core from reset is secure and needs one of the boot
	  backends below.  Without one the driver stages and authenticates the
	  image but cannot start the core.

	  This can be built as a module, which will be called aoc.

config GOOGLE_AOC_TRUSTY
	bool "Start the AOC through the Trusty GSA hardware manager"
	depends on GOOGLE_AOC
	depends on TRUSTY = y || TRUSTY = GOOGLE_AOC
	default y
	help
	  Bring the AOC out of reset with the GSA hardware-manager Trusty
	  application, which programs the core's secure SysMMU context and
	  then releases it.  This is how a stock device starts the AOC, and
	  the mailbox the GSA authenticates the image over cannot do it.

	  Say Y unless you have another way to start the core.

config GOOGLE_AOC_CHANNEL
	tristate "AoC channelized comms (AOCC) in-kernel client"
	depends on GOOGLE_AOC
	help
	  AOCC multiplexes many logical channels over one AOC service.  This
	  provides the in-kernel client used by the sensor hub (USF) and the
	  touch bus negotiator; there is no userspace char device, because
	  those stacks run entirely in the kernel.

	  This can be built as a module, which will be called aoc-channel.

config GOOGLE_AOC_USF
	tristate "Google AoC USF (Unified Sensor Framework) client"
	depends on GOOGLE_AOC_CHANNEL
	help
	  Client session for the sensor-hub firmware running on the AOC: the
	  AOCC transport, the request/response transactions, server-handle
	  resolution, sensor enumeration and sampling control.  Consumers add
	  the policy -- the IIO bridge and the wake-gesture input driver.

	  This can be built as a module, which will be called aoc-usf.
